The specific occupancy-activation mechanism is falsified if provisioning the existing quantile margin makes uncapped occupancy-one links select and decode their target mode. Remaining power-cap outages do not rescue the argument that low occupancy is intrinsically unservable. Positive joint gains, beam evacuations, or positive interaction values alone would not establish that this mechanism survives: interference, discrete mode transitions and shared energy costs can still reward joint reassignment.

The experiment preserves the original selector, which maximizes boundary-zero `F = B − eta_ref E`, with the sealed `eta_ref = 19,720,681.00172232` bit/J. It does not maximize the reported 48-boundary pooled EE ratio. An F improvement therefore need not improve that ratio; even at one boundary, the coefficient would have to equal the reference profile's EE for those improvement tests to coincide. Thus a disappearing or negative gain refutes the original selector's reported +6.359% under margin provisioning, not every possible joint EE optimizer.

The unchanged service rule recomputes its guard from BASELINE under each formulation. It constrains selection-time served count against BASELINE, not against UNILATERAL and not against rate-target satisfaction. This is reoptimization under a changed physical formulation, not rescoring fixed assignments. Consequently the realized candidate catalogues also differ, while their construction rules remain fixed.

At review time, 10 margin anchors yield −0.244636% pooled oracle-over-unilateral EE, with both serving 1,000/1,000 user-anchor endpoints. All eight completed original anchors match archived configurations and committed outcomes exactly; three contain only approximately 10⁻⁵-bit selection-reduction differences. These partial figures must be replaced by the complete panel before reporting a verdict.

